<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arminius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 17 BCE – 21 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arminius was a chieftain of the Germanic Cherusci who had served in the Roman army. In 9 CE he led an alliance of Germanic tribes that destroyed three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-legion">Roman legions</a> under Varus in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>. The defeat halted Roman efforts to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arminius.jpg" alt="Arminius" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daniel Schwen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermannsdenkmal_statue.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Arminius was born in 18 or 17 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, the son of the Cheruscan chief Segimerus, who was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The name Arminius is probably not his Germanic name, but one granted with Roman citizenship. He learned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and, with his younger brother Flavus, joined the Roman army, in which he served between 1 and 6 CE. He received a military education, Roman citizenship and the status of eques, which gave him knowledge of Roman politics and tactics that he later used against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. He returned to northern Germania in 7 or 8 CE, where Rome had secured the lands east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and was seeking to extend its hold to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> under the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/publius-quinctilius-varus">Publius Quinctilius Varus</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Arminius plotted to unite the Germanic tribes against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, a hard task since they were strongly independent and many were enemies of each other. Between 6 and 9 CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> moved eight of the eleven legions east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> to put down a revolt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, leaving Varus with three. In autumn 9 CE Arminius brought Varus a false report of rebellion and persuaded him to divert his legions. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutoburg-forest">Teutoburg Forest</a>, near Kalkriese Hill about 20 kilometres north of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osnabruck">Osnabrück</a>, the Cherusci and their allies ambushed and annihilated the army of over 20,000 men along a narrow forest road. Varus fell on his sword.</p><p>Between 14 and 16 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanicus">Germanicus</a> led punitive campaigns, fighting Arminius to a draw at Pontes Longi and, according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>, defeating him twice. Roman troops recovered two of the three lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">legionary</a> eagles, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> then fixed the frontier at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. In 15 Germanicus captured Arminius's wife Thusnelda while she was pregnant.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arminius was murdered in 21 by opponents within his own tribe, who felt he was growing too powerful. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> called him without doubt the liberator of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>. Rome made no further concerted effort to hold Germania beyond the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, and modern historians count his victory among the most decisive battles in history. The rediscovery of Tacitus made him a symbol of German national identity, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> possibly first called him Hermann. Heinrich von Kleist wrote the play Die Hermannsschlacht in 1808, and the Hermannsdenkmal near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/detmold">Detmold</a>, begun in 1838, was dedicated after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arminius” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 17 CE war broke out between the alliances of <b>Arminius</b> and Maroboduus, and after an indecisive battle Maroboduus withdrew into Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marcomanni">Marcomanni · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Rhine-Danube frontier, fixed after <b>Arminius</b> ambushed three legions under Publius Quinctilius Varus in the Teutoburg Forest, marked the northern limit of Roman expansion, with lasting effects on European history.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Arminius</b> led Germanic warriors in an ambush that wiped out three legions under Varus over several days, by tradition ending around this date.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-9">9 September in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 9 CE they joined <b>Arminius</b> against Varus and captured the eagle of the Nineteenth Legion, which Germanicus' general Stertinius recovered in 15.</blockquote><a href="/state/bructeri">Bructeri</a></li><li><blockquote>They supported Rome against <b>Arminius</b>, but kept their independence after Roman forces withdrew from Germany.</blockquote><a href="/state/chauci">Chauci</a></li><li><blockquote>After the campaigns of Drusus from 12 BCE they became Roman allies, and their noble sons, among them <b>Arminius</b>, served in the Roman army and received Roman citizenship.</blockquote><a href="/state/cherusci">Cherusci</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weser">Weser</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=7" aria-label="Germania on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Arminius"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5595 5725h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5595 5725h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=50.9,11.1&amp;year=7">What was here in 7 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Not by treachery, not against pregnant women, but openly against armed men do I wage war. There are still to be seen in the groves of Germany the Roman standards which I hung up to our country's gods.&#8221;</p><footer>Tacitus, Annals, book 1 &#183; tr. A. J. Church and W. J. Brodribb, 1876 &#183; after the Romans captured his wife; Tacitus' account &#183; <a href="https://classics.mit.edu/Tacitus/annals.mb.txt" rel="noopener">The Internet Classics Archive</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
